---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'PROFILE', title: 'Google Business Profile', text: 'Category, services, photos, posts and reviews. This is the main field of the race on the map.' },
  { label: 'SITE', title: 'Your site', text: 'Google also looks at the site your profile links to. We make it clear on your site which service you offer in which district.' },
  { label: 'CONSISTENCY', title: 'Name, address, phone', text: 'The same spelling in every directory that lists you. Inconsistent details lower Google’s trust in you.' },
  { label: 'MEASUREMENT', title: 'Measurement across your area', text: 'We measure where you rank from different corners around you, because the order changes when the searcher moves two streets away.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'nedir', label: 'What does local SEO do?' },
  { id: 'fark', label: 'How it differs from regular SEO' },
  { id: 'nap', label: 'Name, address and phone' },
  { id: 'site', label: 'How your site affects the map' },
  { id: 'coklu', label: 'Branches and cities' },
  { id: 'program', label: 'The 12-week program' },
  { id: 'olcum', label: 'Measurement and reporting' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What’s the difference between local SEO and regular SEO?',
    a: 'Regular SEO works toward a single results list. Local SEO has two races: the map with three businesses at the top, and the regular results below it. On the map, your business profile and how close you are to the searcher decide the order.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When will I see results?',
    a: 'We can’t give you a timeframe; it depends on where your business starts, on the competition and on the channel. What we can tell you is the order of the work: we measure first, fix the root problems, then produce. You see what we did and how the measurement changed in your weekly report.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you guarantee rankings?',
    a: 'No, because no one can guarantee rankings. We put forward the work we do and the measurement: you see in the report what we did and how your ranking changed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I do it myself?',
    a: 'You can make the basic fixes on the profile yourself. The hard part is keeping the review flow alive and measuring what changes in your area on a regular basis; most businesses lose that rhythm. If you want to start, the free profile analysis tool on our site shows what’s missing.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/local-seo"
  title="Local SEO Service | Wiserrise"
  description="We fix your profile and your site together so nearby customers find you on Google Maps. Measurement across your area, weekly report."
  eyebrow="SERVICE · LOCAL SEO"
  h1="Stand out on the map with local SEO."
  crumb="Local SEO"
  lede="When someone types “physiotherapist near me” on their phone, Google first shows the map and three businesses. Local SEO is the work of fixing your Google Business Profile and your site together to get into those three. We start with measurement across your area, then close the gaps one by one."
  serviceName="Local SEO"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp'), text: 'We manage your category, service structure, reviews and posts together.' },
    { label: 'Map Rank Tracking', href: p('services/rank-tracking'), text: 'We measure where you rank from different points around you.' },
    { label: 'Technical SEO', href: p('services/technical-seo'), text: 'We fix crawling and indexing errors so your site opens properly to Google.' },
    { label: 'GEO', href: p('services/geo'), text: 'We work on getting you mentioned in ChatGPT, Gemini and Google’s AI answers.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="nedir">What does local SEO do?</h2>

  <p>
    It brings nearby customers to you. When someone in Leeds searches for “dentist”, Google shows two lists: the map
    with three businesses at the top, and the regular search results below. Most of the clicks go to the top three.
  </p>

  <p>
    The two lists are ranked by different rules. On the map your <a href={p('services/gbp')}>Google Business Profile</a>
    decides; in the list below, your site does. We run both as parts of the same job, because Google looks at the same
    business in both lists and one feeds the other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fark">How is it different from regular SEO?</h2>

  <p>
    In regular SEO you compete for a single list. Local SEO has two lists, and where the searcher stands changes the order.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Regular SEO</th>
          <th>Local SEO</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Where you compete</strong></td>
          <td>A single results list</td>
          <td>The map and the list below it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Effect of location</strong></td>
          <td>Small</td>
          <td>Decisive. The order changes when the searcher moves two streets away.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Main asset</strong></td>
          <td>Site</td>
          <td>Business profile and site</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Measurement</strong></td>
          <td>A single rank</td>
          <td>Your rank at different points in your area</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>How the customer arrives</strong></td>
          <td>Visits the site</td>
          <td>Calls, gets directions, sends a message. May never visit the site.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Don’t skip the last row. Some of the customers who find you on the map call you without ever opening your site. A
    business that only looks at its site statistics sees less of its map business than it really gets.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nap">Why should your name, address and phone be the same everywhere?</h2>

  <p>
    Google wants to recognize you as the same business everywhere it sees you. We call this NAP: name, address, phone.
  </p>

  <p>
    If one directory says “Bluefern Physiotherapy”, another says “Blue Fern Physio” and a third still shows your old
    number, Google’s trust weakens. We list every place your name appears and fix it to a single spelling. Industry
    directories, map services, social profiles and your old site, if you have one, are all part of this.
  </p>

  <p>
    For businesses that work without showing an address, the address field stays empty. Then it matters even more that your
    name and phone are exactly the same everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">Does your site affect your place on the map?</h2>

  <p>
    It does. Google also looks at the site your profile links to: which services you describe and which districts you name.
    If a service has no page, you’ll struggle with it on the map too.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where competition is strong, the profile alone isn’t enough. First we make sure your site opens properly to Google with
    <a href={p('services/technical-seo')}>technical SEO</a>. Then we fit each page to a single search
    (<a href={p('services/on-page-seo')} style="white-space: nowrap;">on-page SEO</a>) and write the missing service pages
    (<a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a>).
  </p>

  <p>
    We learned this the hard way. One client didn’t want to hand their site over to us. We sent the to-do list to their
    developer as .md files, and the developer fed them to their own AI tool.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pages that came out were terrible, and for months we did the same work over and over. In the end we took over the
    whole site, and fixing it took at least a month. Since then our rule is clear: if we can’t manage the whole site, we
    don’t start.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coklu">What if you have more than one branch or city?</h2>

  <p>
    Each branch counts as a separate business. Each branch gets its own profile and its own page on the site. Branch pages
    that are copies of each other are the most common mistake we see, and the most expensive one.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you have no branches but serve several cities, don’t open extra profiles; it’s against Google’s rules. In that case
    we build your visibility in those cities on the site. Google treats copied pages with only the city name changed as
    “doorway” pages, and they harm the site.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Before you open a city page, ask yourself:</strong> Do I have something real to say about that city? If you
      have no work from there and no information specific to that area, don’t open the page. Ten good city pages do more
      than fifty copied ones.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="program">What do we do in the 12-week program?</h2>

  <p>We follow the same order with every business. We don’t touch anything before we measure.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 1-2, measurement.</strong> We take a baseline measurement across your area. We map out your profile,
      the category and review gap between you and your competitors, your site’s technical state and the directories where
      your name appears.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 3-4, root fixes.</strong> We fix the category and service structure, then clear out the errors that
      keep your site from opening to Google and the inconsistent listings in directories. Most of the work comes out in
      these two weeks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 5-8, production.</strong> We write the missing service pages. The profile post calendar and the review
      request flow start.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 9-12, tightening.</strong> We push on the areas that lag behind in the measurement. We tidy up the
      internal links on the site and respond to competitors’ moves.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The work doesn’t end when the 12-week program is over. Your competitors are working too, and if the flow of reviews and posts
    stops, your ranking slips back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="olcum">What do we measure and report?</h2>

  <p>
    We don’t settle for a single ranking screenshot. Your place on the map changes with where the searcher stands, and a
    measurement from one point won’t show you the real picture.
  </p>

  <p>That’s why we read four sources together:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>
      <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>Measurement across your area</a>: where you rank from different points around
      you and how that changes over time
    </li>
    <li>Profile interactions: how many people found you, got directions, called or sent a message</li>
    <li>Search Console: which searches your site appears in and how many clicks it gets</li>
    <li>Our work: what we changed on the profile, on the site and in directories</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    You see the work we’ve done in the report every week. Reading all four together, we catch situations like “the ranking
    went up but the phone isn’t ringing” early and change the plan to match.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>What’s your <span class="highlight">rank</span> in your city right now?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within
      <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
